# WHAT A BROWSER MAY KEEP, AND FOR HOW LONG.
#
# ⚠️ A CONTENT-HASHED FILE WAS BEING REVALIDATED ON EVERY VISIT. Cloudflare's
# static assets default to `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` for everything,
# and this deployment took the default — so a phone opening the product asked
# the server about all eighteen hashed files in `/assets/` before it could run
# any of them: the entry bundle, the stylesheet, five Geist weights, three
# Onest, and every lazily-loaded chunk. Eighteen conditional round trips, on
# every visit, for ever, and every one of them answered "yes, still the same".
#
# ⚠️ THE HASH IN THE NAME IS THE WHOLE POINT. Vite writes the content's
# fingerprint into the filename, so `index-Bf05fZYn.js` cannot change meaning:
# a new build is a new NAME, referenced from a fresh `index.html`. A file that
# cannot change is a file there is nothing to ask about, and `immutable` is how
# a browser is told not to ask — not even on a reload, which is the one case
# `max-age` alone does not cover and the one the person filming was doing.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE DOCUMENT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE. `index.html` names which
# hashed files this deploy uses, so caching it would pin a browser to the
# previous build's names — the mechanism in `runtime/renewal.ts` reads that same
# document to report which version is being served. It keeps the default, which
# is to revalidate: one round trip, on a 5 KB file, that is the only thing
# standing between somebody and the current build. `sw.js` is unhashed for the
# same reason and keeps the same treatment.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
